    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 6\n    nc = 8\n    facility_coords = [(14, 97), (2, 30), (33, 53), (71, 15), (15, 87), (46, 38)]\n    customer_coords = [(51, 11), (97, 46), (30, 99), (40, 76), (82, 37), (26, 19), (61, 12), (89, 36)]\n    capacities = [188.0, 58.0, 113.0, 72.0, 200.0, 112.0]\n    demands = [35.0, 47.0, 41.0, 21.0, 29.0, 47.0, 23.0, 24.0]\n    fixed_costs = [298.0, 449.0, 431.0, 220.0, 316.0, 392.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: no facility serves > 61% of total demand\n    total_demand = 267.0\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= 0.61*total_demand)\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1583\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1583.0)\n\n    # Policy: F4 requires F3\n    m.addConstr(open_f[4] <= open_f[3])\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Distribution Network Activation and Fulfillment Plan\nDate: [Insert Date]\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework to select which candidate distribution centers to activate and how to route shipments so every customer\u2019s demand is met at the lowest total cost. The analysis considers the fixed cost to open facilities, the variable cost of shipping based on distance and volume, and operational limits at each site and customer location. The facility and customer locations, capacities, demand volumes, and rate information are detailed in the Annex.\n\nDecision Scope and Operating Requirements\n- Service coverage: Every customer location listed in the data pack must have its full demand satisfied through shipments from the activated facilities.\n- Activation logic: Opening a facility requires a fixed activation outlay. Facilities that are not activated may not ship product or serve demand.\n- Capacity discipline: Each activated facility can ship only up to its designated capacity.\n- Transportation spending: Shipment spend is driven by the distance between the origin facility and destination customer and the units moved, using the transport rate referenced in the Annex; distances will be derived from the provided map coordinates.\n- Cost objective: The plan should minimize total cost, combining the fixed activation outlays with the transportation spend needed to move product to customers.\n\nMandatory Company Policies\n- Risk diversification: To prevent over-reliance on a single asset, no distribution center may fulfill more than 61% of total system demand across all customers.\n- Capital allocation: The total fixed activation spend for all selected facilities may not exceed $1,583.\n- Operating dependency: The site identified as F4 (15, 87) relies on the regional hub F3 (71, 15). If F4 is activated, F3 must also be activated. Activating F3 without F4 is permitted.\n\nDecision Outputs\n- A recommended list of facilities to activate that complies with the capital budget and dependency policy.\n- A shipment plan indicating which customer volumes will be served from each activated site, such that every customer\u2019s demand is fulfilled without exceeding any site\u2019s capacity and without allowing any single site to exceed the 61% share of total demand.\n- A total cost estimate combining activation and transportation spend, with clear traceability back to the inputs in the Annex.\n\nImplementation Notes\n- The facility and customer coordinates provided will be used to determine distances for shipping spend calculations; no shipments will be planned from unactivated sites.\n- The chosen activation set will be verified against the capital budget cap and the F4\u2013F3 dependency before finalization.\n- The shipment plan will be validated for full demand coverage, capacity adherence, and the 61% site share policy prior to execution.\n\nAnnex \u2014 Data Pack (to be used for modeling and cost estimation)\n- Facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (14, 97)\n  - F1: (2, 30)\n  - F2: (33, 53)\n  - F3: (71, 15)\n  - F4: (15, 87)\n  - F5: (46, 38)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (51, 11)\n  - C1: (97, 46)\n  - C2: (30, 99)\n  - C3: (40, 76)\n  - C4: (82, 37)\n  - C5: (26, 19)\n  - C6: (61, 12)\n  - C7: (89, 36)\n- Facility capacities: [188.0, 58.0, 113.0, 72.0, 200.0, 112.0]\n- Customer demands: [35.0, 47.0, 41.0, 21.0, 29.0, 47.0, 23.0, 24.0]\n- Fixed activation costs: [298.0, 449.0, 431.0, 220.0, 316.0, 392.0]\n- Transport rate (per kilometer per unit): 0.5",
